Caller owns `Run`, aggregate lifecycle and `Evidence` across failures. `Error { kind, attempted_refs }` retains typed publisher attempts; raw registered/pending objects remain in Evidence. A capture error retains its existing rich partial-publication error. No rollback is claimed.

## Exact boundaries

* The result subject is the frozen baseline/repaired source **before verification**, never compiler output or scratch. Seven role subjects are checked with `subjects::verify` before and after collection. Baseline requires exact empty patch and same source inventory/content. Repair requires the existing reference.patch SHA `12db53d34f3a388d19d1b971d2bd5dfeb67f8c0e1a459d25e1012954c7b22f8e`, the fixed base/reference lib.rs bytes, and unchanged other source content; existing `app::repair` owns effects.
* Oracle accepts only ORACLE-U64-001/v1 SHA `9354a52b2539e562ec8c3b957dbe693d75411e29c88d19a446b7cc488862b427`; wrapper SHA is `2fb599c68f57cb8b777f595f1217762531f9c6784c72765d15e83c553b4c5f59`. Both plus frozen recipe are bound into the reviewed expectation graph.
* Actual ordered stages are `compile-library`, `link-driver`, `execute-driver`. Native and launcher status stay separate. Completed prefixes can retain producer failure/nonexecution; `Step::Refused` or an empty run has insufficient capture support and refuses root assembly, retaining caller-owned Run and unresolved cleanup. It cannot become a clean stop.
* Actual namespace before/inside/after input maps must match frozen runtime/tool pins plus the exact stage source, wrapper, rlib, driver and public input projection. Captured rlib/driver bytes are published as separate artifacts. Missing/changed binding blocks PASS. Resource scope, exact 4GiB scratch and namespace protection/descriptors must have actual readback; unbounded workload collection cannot acquire PASS here.
* All three absolute Clock deadlines must project to the frozen recipe from one origin. Actual candidate timeout selects the frozen candidate cutoff for the existing decision API; otherwise it selects the coordinator verification cutoff. Raw decision evidence names that selected phase. Actual interruption/durable cancellation timestamps are retained, never replaced by the cutoff. The process report end is a conservative sample, not an exact native exit timestamp. Oracle checked_at is sampled on the actual coordinator-local check. No refreshed deadline is introduced.
* `capture::capture` correctly reports diagnostic counting unmeasured. The bridge's fixed baseline only measures zero diagnostics when compiler stdout and all candidate/launcher stderr are complete and empty. Nonempty/partial diagnostic bytes remain retained with unavailable counting and block PASS; no artificial warning/error counts.
* One reviewed workload case contains 335 vector observations. They are never 335 engine cases. Ordinary valid mismatch yields FAIL; decision policy handles broken evidence, native errors, causal stop and cleanup separately. The baseline does not redefine mismatch as an expected negative producer pass.
* Cleanup is supplied by the trusted aggregate owner with its actual readback payload; bridge combines it with direct process/channel/scratch observations and material obligation rows. Payload hashes do not authenticate observations. Resource numeric readings remain in raw namespace capture facts; an empty typed resource list does not claim a complete quantitative metric inventory.

## Production preparation still required

Old `workload-bounded` runs showed real base mismatch/reference agreement but had no pre-execution u64 RC04 expectation/review graph. They cannot be retroactively given one. The example preparation entry creates the exact real expectation graph and review request only; it launches no producer, runs no comparison, and authors no review. A production front end still supplies the exact seven subjects, tool/host/lock/profile/standards closure, independently accepted case review, actual clocks and aggregate evidence before a fresh run can qualify for an RC04 candidate pass.

The fixed public wrapper shares a process with candidate library code and cannot authenticate hostile same-process output. Finite black-box agreement, exact metadata consistency and local TH-DEV custody are not hostile containment, module admission or task acceptance. No scope broadening is claimed.

## Integration

Copy the four source files preserving subdirectories. The private harness imports `habitat_engine::`; integration inside that crate changes those imports to `crate::` and adds `pub mod u64_receipt;` under app. No dependency changes. The small developer controls are separate from independent qualification; exact source/outcome hashes are retained in the release evidence.

## Released verification and remaining execution coverage

Seven developer-authored preparation controls passed against the copied source subject: exact oracle/wrapper/recipe closure, altered oracle, changed wrapper, invalid cutoff ordering, expired preparation, mutation after snapshot capture, and closed recipe decoding. All-target pedantic Clippy and pinned rustfmt checks exited0. Initial module-path and pedantic failures are retained; no test result was relabeled. The actual example published a five-object expectation graph and review request without regenerating it afterward.

The full `prepare → actual Run → observe → finalize` path has not executed in this private release. The parent owns fresh reviewed runtime integration and execution. In particular, the trusted front end must construct tool/host/profile/support records from the same actual configuration supplied to `Preparation`; closed metadata alone cannot establish that provenance. The bridge verifies frozen source-role contents and actual stage pins, and does not invent host or aggregate measurements.
